WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.515
Shivaar, this is lesson one in the new
style: RecyclerView, pointer four.

00:00:06.635 --> 00:00:10.236
First the slides and the study
guide, in plain words.

00:00:10.356 --> 00:00:13.251
Then the whiteboard, every line
explained.

00:00:13.371 --> 00:00:15.091
Then a real project,

00:00:15.211 --> 00:00:18.233
built and run on a real Android
emulator.

00:00:18.353 --> 00:00:22.020
Then three classic mistakes, run
for real and fixed.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.972
Then your turn: two TODO
questions.

00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:30.232
Slide three of Architecting
Android Lists: the four pillars.

00:00:30.352 --> 00:00:34.855
The RecyclerView is the display
glass: a widget in your XML.

00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:36.992
The Adapter is the engine:

00:00:37.112 --> 00:00:40.522
a Java class that connects your
ArrayList to the rows.

00:00:40.642 --> 00:00:43.044
The ViewHolder is the
scaffolding:

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:45.587
it remembers one row's widgets.

00:00:45.707 --> 00:00:48.451
The LayoutManager is the site
manager:

00:00:48.571 --> 00:00:51.337
it stacks the rows as a list or
a grid.

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:54.594
The study guide says the same,

00:00:54.714 --> 00:01:00.073
on pages one hundred and fourteen to
one hundred and fifteen.

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.451
Almost all the list's behaviour
lives in the Adapter.

00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:06.188
The ViewHolder belongs to the
Adapter.

00:01:06.308 --> 00:01:09.760
And the LayoutManager measures
and positions the rows,

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.755
and decides when a row can be
recycled.

00:01:13.908 --> 00:01:18.024
Slide four: component one, the
XML blueprint.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.824
This file describes one row
only.

00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:23.625
Android stamps it out again and
again,

00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:26.210
once for every item in your
list.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.426
Slide five is the trap.

00:01:29.546 --> 00:01:32.248
If the row's root height is
match_parent,

00:01:32.368 --> 00:01:36.522
one row claims the whole screen.
Use wrap_content.

00:01:36.642 --> 00:01:40.737
The study guide, pages one hundred
and sixteen to seventeen,

00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:42.980
calls this very important.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.573
We'll make this exact mistake
later, on a real phone.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.301
Slide seven: component two, the
ViewHolder.

00:01:51.421 --> 00:01:54.531
Its constructor calls super with
the row's view,

00:01:54.651 --> 00:01:58.403
then findViewById, once, on
itemView.

00:01:58.523 --> 00:02:01.031
Searching the XML is slow,

00:02:01.151 --> 00:02:06.693
so the ViewHolder caches the result
and scrolling stays smooth.

00:02:07.825 --> 00:02:12.219
Slide eight: component three,
the Adapter, with three gears.

00:02:12.339 --> 00:02:15.104
getItemCount says how many rows.

00:02:15.224 --> 00:02:18.356
onCreateViewHolder builds an
empty row shell.

00:02:18.476 --> 00:02:21.520
onBindViewHolder fills a shell
with data.

00:02:22.658 --> 00:02:25.360
Slide nine shows two gears in
code.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.375
getItemCount returns the list's
size.

00:02:28.495 --> 00:02:32.032
onCreateViewHolder inflates the
row XML,

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:36.310
with parent and false, then
returns a new ContactViewHolder.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.637
Slide ten, gear three.

00:02:39.757 --> 00:02:43.037
onBindViewHolder gets a holder
and a position.

00:02:43.157 --> 00:02:45.964
Cast the holder to
ContactViewHolder,

00:02:46.084 --> 00:02:48.465
get the Contact at that
position,

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.371
and set its name on the cached
TextView.

00:02:52.492 --> 00:02:56.438
Here is the study guide's
version: Listing six point two,

00:02:56.558 --> 00:02:59.153
on page one hundred and
seventeen.

00:02:59.273 --> 00:03:01.097
The same three methods,

00:03:01.217 --> 00:03:03.746
with the ViewHolder as an inner
class.

00:03:03.866 --> 00:03:07.488
Page one hundred and eighteen
explains it line by line.

00:03:07.608 --> 00:03:13.439
getItemCount decides how many
times the other two methods run.

00:03:14.575 --> 00:03:17.298
Slide twelve: the LayoutManager.

00:03:17.418 --> 00:03:20.528
LinearLayoutManager gives a
vertical list.

00:03:20.648 --> 00:03:24.658
GridLayoutManager, with this and
two, gives two columns,

00:03:24.778 --> 00:03:26.837
without touching the Adapter.

00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:32.150
Slide thirteen wires it all in
the Activity, in four steps.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.220
Find the RecyclerView.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.291
Set the LayoutManager.

00:03:36.411 --> 00:03:38.792
Create the Adapter with the
data.

00:03:38.912 --> 00:03:40.603
Set the Adapter.

00:03:40.723 --> 00:03:43.682
The study guide's Listing six
point three,

00:03:43.802 --> 00:03:46.718
pages one hundred and eighteen
to nineteen,

00:03:46.838 --> 00:03:48.746
does the same four steps.

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:51.545
Now the whiteboard.

00:03:51.665 --> 00:03:56.316
Step one: the row blueprint,
simple_item_view dot XML,

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:58.517
the study guide's file name.

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.824
The root is a LinearLayout.

00:04:00.944 --> 00:04:04.675
Width match_parent, so the row
is as wide as the list.

00:04:04.795 --> 00:04:09.628
Height wrap_content: never
match_parent. Two TextViews.

00:04:09.748 --> 00:04:13.737
At plus id creates the ids the
ViewHolder will look for:

00:04:13.857 --> 00:04:16.452
textViewName and textViewPhone.

00:04:16.572 --> 00:04:21.051
Sizes in sp, because sp follows
the user's font setting.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.294
Step two: the screen,
activity_main dot XML.

00:04:26.414 --> 00:04:29.116
A ConstraintLayout that fills
the screen.

00:04:29.236 --> 00:04:33.073
Inside, the RecyclerView, with
its full class name,

00:04:33.193 --> 00:04:36.237
androidx dot recyclerview dot
widget.

00:04:36.357 --> 00:04:38.910
Its id is rvContacts.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.482
Width and height zero dp mean
match constraint:

00:04:42.602 --> 00:04:47.910
stretch between the four constraints
that pin each edge to the parent.

00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:52.549
Step three: the model, Contact
dot java.

00:04:52.669 --> 00:04:56.785
One Contact holds one row's
data: a name and a phone number.

00:04:56.905 --> 00:05:00.506
The constructor stores them, and
the getters hand them back.

00:05:00.626 --> 00:05:03.349
onBindViewHolder will call
getName.

00:05:04.492 --> 00:05:06.721
Step four: the Adapter.

00:05:06.841 --> 00:05:09.694
It extends RecyclerView dot
Adapter,

00:05:09.814 --> 00:05:12.258
and holds an ArrayList of
Contact.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:14.695
Inside it, the ViewHolder:

00:05:14.815 --> 00:05:18.588
an inner class that extends
RecyclerView dot ViewHolder.

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:22.439
Its constructor gets itemView,
one inflated row.

00:05:22.559 --> 00:05:26.739
Call super first, then
findViewById on itemView,

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:28.573
not on the Activity.

00:05:28.693 --> 00:05:34.093
The Adapter's own constructor
receives the list and keeps it.

00:05:35.242 --> 00:05:39.823
Step five: the three methods.
onCreateViewHolder:

00:05:39.943 --> 00:05:43.029
LayoutInflater from the parent's
context,

00:05:43.149 --> 00:05:46.559
inflate the row XML with parent
and false,

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.511
and wrap it in a new
ContactViewHolder.

00:05:49.631 --> 00:05:53.725
onBindViewHolder: cast the
holder to ContactViewHolder,

00:05:53.845 --> 00:05:57.489
get the Contact at position, and
set the name and the phone.

00:05:57.609 --> 00:06:01.424
getItemCount: return contactData
dot size.

00:06:01.544 --> 00:06:04.352
An ArrayList has size, not
length.

00:06:05.492 --> 00:06:08.493
Step six: wire it up in
onCreate.

00:06:08.613 --> 00:06:13.243
super, then setContentView,
before any findViewById.

00:06:13.363 --> 00:06:15.723
Build the ArrayList of contacts.

00:06:15.843 --> 00:06:20.724
Then the four lines from slide
thirteen. Find rvContacts.

00:06:20.844 --> 00:06:23.609
Set a LinearLayoutManager with
this.

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:26.815
Create the ContactAdapter with
the contacts.

00:06:26.935 --> 00:06:28.626
Set the Adapter.

00:06:29.783 --> 00:06:31.463
Now the real thing.

00:06:31.583 --> 00:06:33.685
This is the actual project,

00:06:33.805 --> 00:06:36.637
in the standard Android Studio
folders.

00:06:36.757 --> 00:06:40.724
Java files under app, source,
main, java.

00:06:40.844 --> 00:06:43.418
Layouts under res, layout.

00:06:43.538 --> 00:06:47.354
Every line has a comment, so you
can read it on your laptop.

00:06:48.492 --> 00:06:51.772
Here are the Adapter's three
methods in the real file,

00:06:51.892 --> 00:06:54.100
exactly as on the whiteboard.

00:06:55.242 --> 00:06:57.943
Here is MainActivity in the
project,

00:06:58.063 --> 00:07:00.959
with the same four wiring lines
highlighted.

00:07:02.117 --> 00:07:05.333
Gradle builds it for real: build
successful.

00:07:05.453 --> 00:07:08.563
Then adb installs the app on the
emulator,

00:07:08.683 --> 00:07:12.992
a Pixel six running Android
fourteen, API thirty-four.

00:07:14.117 --> 00:07:17.717
And here it runs. Fifteen
contacts.

00:07:17.837 --> 00:07:20.581
Each row is one copy of the
blueprint,

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:23.297
filled in by onBindViewHolder.

00:07:23.417 --> 00:07:25.540
I scroll down and back up.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:30.965
Rows that leave the screen are
reused for the rows coming in.

00:07:31.085 --> 00:07:33.893
That's the recycling in
RecyclerView.

00:07:35.033 --> 00:07:38.186
Mistake one: forget
setLayoutManager.

00:07:38.306 --> 00:07:42.422
It still compiles, and it still
runs, but the list is empty.

00:07:42.542 --> 00:07:47.975
Logcat gives the clue: No layout
manager attached, skipping layout.

00:07:48.095 --> 00:07:50.370
In the exam there's no logcat,

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.385
so always check all four wiring
lines.

00:07:54.533 --> 00:07:56.914
The fix: put the line back.

00:07:57.034 --> 00:07:59.820
Rebuild, reinstall, rerun.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.631
The list is back.

00:08:02.783 --> 00:08:06.536
Mistake two: the wrap_content
trap from slide five.

00:08:06.656 --> 00:08:09.209
The row's root height is
match_parent.

00:08:09.329 --> 00:08:12.415
It runs, but each row fills the
whole screen.

00:08:12.535 --> 00:08:15.494
I scroll, and there's one
contact per screen.

00:08:16.617 --> 00:08:19.703
The fix: wrap_content on the
row's root.

00:08:19.823 --> 00:08:23.166
Rebuild, rerun, and many rows
fit again.

00:08:24.325 --> 00:08:27.883
Mistake three: inflate with true
instead of false.

00:08:28.003 --> 00:08:31.220
It compiles, but the app crashes
on launch:

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.564
My Contact List keeps stopping.
Logcat says:

00:08:35.684 --> 00:08:39.157
ViewHolder views must not be
attached when created.

00:08:39.277 --> 00:08:42.878
RecyclerView attaches the row
itself, later.

00:08:44.033 --> 00:08:45.878
The fix: false.

00:08:45.998 --> 00:08:48.742
Rebuild, rerun, and it works.

00:08:49.867 --> 00:08:51.544
Key takeaways.

00:08:51.664 --> 00:08:53.344
Pause and copy them.

00:08:53.464 --> 00:08:57.752
Four parts: glass, engine,
cache, site manager.

00:08:57.872 --> 00:08:59.889
Row height wrap_content.

00:09:00.009 --> 00:09:01.833
Find on itemView.

00:09:01.953 --> 00:09:04.333
Inflate with parent and false.

00:09:04.453 --> 00:09:06.704
Cast, then get position.

00:09:06.824 --> 00:09:09.247
getItemCount returns size.

00:09:09.367 --> 00:09:15.615
And four wiring lines: find,
LayoutManager, Adapter, setAdapter.

00:09:16.742 --> 00:09:18.433
Your turn.

00:09:18.553 --> 00:09:24.208
Question one, Java: complete the
ProductAdapter. Four TODOs.

00:09:24.328 --> 00:09:26.923
Pause the video and write it on
paper.

00:09:48.075 --> 00:09:52.384
Question two, XML and Java:
write the row layout,

00:09:52.504 --> 00:09:55.827
then wire the RecyclerView as a
two-column grid.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.641
Answer one.

00:10:18.761 --> 00:10:23.755
TODO one: findViewById on
itemView, for both TextViews.

00:10:23.875 --> 00:10:27.948
TODO two: inflate item_product
with parent and false,

00:10:28.068 --> 00:10:30.748
and return a new
ProductViewHolder.

00:10:30.868 --> 00:10:34.899
TODO three: cast the holder, get
the product at position,

00:10:35.019 --> 00:10:37.999
set the name, and set the price
as a String.

00:10:38.119 --> 00:10:40.221
setText needs text.

00:10:40.341 --> 00:10:44.008
A bare int is treated as a
resource id and crashes.

00:10:44.128 --> 00:10:47.538
TODO four: return productData
dot size.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:50.378
Answer two.

00:10:50.498 --> 00:10:53.651
The root LinearLayout:
match_parent width,

00:10:53.771 --> 00:10:56.472
wrap_content height, horizontal.

00:10:56.592 --> 00:10:59.851
The name: width zero dp and
weight one,

00:10:59.971 --> 00:11:02.330
so it takes the space left over.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.746
The price: wrap_content.

00:11:04.866 --> 00:11:07.010
Then find rvProducts,

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:10.153
set a GridLayoutManager with
this and two,

00:11:10.273 --> 00:11:12.569
and set a new ProductAdapter.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:16.701
Both answers were built and run
on the emulator:

00:11:16.821 --> 00:11:20.936
a two-column grid of products.
Mark yourself.

00:11:22.075 --> 00:11:23.899
That's RecyclerView.

00:11:24.019 --> 00:11:27.362
Open the project in Android
Studio, run it,

00:11:27.482 --> 00:11:30.377
and break it on purpose, the way
we did.

00:11:30.497 --> 00:11:33.263
Then send me photos of your two
answers,

00:11:33.383 --> 00:11:35.527
and I'll mark them line by line.

00:11:35.647 --> 00:11:40.192
Next lesson: Fragments,
DialogFragment and the manifest.

00:11:40.312 --> 00:11:42.263
You've got this, Shivaar.
